WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.383
flatMap takes a function that returns a publisher, called the inner, like this Mono from a fraud service.

2
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:15.748
For each id, flatMap subscribes to the inner right away, without waiting for earlier ones to finish.

3
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:20.267
Three ids arrive together: 3, 1 and 2.

4
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:27.375
Id 3 takes 300 milliseconds to score, id 1 takes 100, and id 2 takes 200.

5
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.132
flatMap subscribes to all three at time zero, so the three calls run at the same time.

6
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.367
Call 1 finishes first, and flatMap passes it on at once.

7
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:38.483
Then 2, then 3.

8
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.310
The output is 1, 2, 3.

9
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:48.505
flatMap merges results in the order they arrive, not in input order, and everything is done at 300 milliseconds.

10
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:50.699
flatMap creates no threads.

11
00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:57.323
These calls overlap only because each inner is asynchronous, like a non-blocking HTTP call.

12
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:01.266
By default, up to 256 inners run at once.

13
00:01:01.266 --> 00:01:05.777
When the calls hit a shared service, pass a limit, such as 4.

14
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.004
The limit is also its demand.

15
00:01:08.004 --> 00:01:13.672
flatMap asks the source for four ids, then for one more each time a call finishes.

16
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.494
When order matters, concatMap subscribes to one inner at a time.

17
00:01:18.494 --> 00:01:22.364
It asks for the next id only when the current inner completes.

18
00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:25.713
3 runs first, for 300 milliseconds.

19
00:01:25.713 --> 00:01:27.119
Then 1, then 2.

20
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.305
Input order: 3, 1, 2.

21
00:01:32.305 --> 00:01:36.675
But the times add up, so it is done at 600 milliseconds.

22
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:44.839
flatMapSequential subscribes to all three at once, like flatMap, but it hands the results on in input order.

23
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:47.915
1 and 2 finish early, so they wait in a buffer.

24
00:01:47.915 --> 00:01:50.707
When 3 finishes, all three go out, in order.

25
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.459
Input order, and done at 300 milliseconds.

26
00:01:54.459 --> 00:02:00.576
The price is memory for the buffer, and one slow first call holds back everything behind it.

27
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.434
Same input, three operators.

28
00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:10.132
Use concatMap when each call must wait for the previous one, like postings to one ledger account.

29
00:02:10.333 --> 00:02:12.971
Kotlin Flow starts from the other side.

30
00:02:12.971 --> 00:02:15.947
It is sequential by default, like concatMap.

31
00:02:15.947 --> 00:02:21.020
You opt in to overlap with flatMapMerge, and Flow has no flatMapSequential.
